'''vulturine''' (vul′tụ̄r-in), ''a.'' [< L. ''vulturinus'', of or pertaining to a vulture, < ''vultur'', a vulture, see ''vulture''.] '''1.''' Resembling a vulture; of or pertaining to the ''Vulturinæ''.—'''2.''' Characteristic of a vulture, as in scenting carrion. Also ''vulturish''.
{{smaller block|The ''vulturine'' nose, which smells nothing but corruption, is no credit to its possessor.
{{right|''Kingsley'', Two Years Ago, x.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Vulturine eagle''', ''Aquila verreauxi'', of Lesson, described also the same year (1830) as ''Aquila vulturina'' by Sir A. Smith. This is an African eagle, 3 feet long, with the feet feathered to the toes, and otherwise congeneric with the golden eagle. When adult it is black, more or less extensively white on the back, rump, and upper tail-coverts;}}
